{"title":"Carmichael function","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><p>There are two different <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Function\" title=\"Function\">functions</a> called the <b>Carmichael function</b>.  Both are similar to <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Euler%27s_totient_function\" title=\"Euler&#39;s totient function\">Euler's totient function</a> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/c/1/2c175f60eecef1de7560c3bdea495d69f26f719d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\phi$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"13\" height=\"18\" />.\n</p>\n<div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#First_Definition\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">First Definition</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Examples\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Examples</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Second_Definition\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Second Definition</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#See_also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"First_Definition\">First Definition</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Carmichael_function&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: First Definition\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>The Carmichael function <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/e/4/ce4588fd900d02afcbd260bc07f54cce49a7dc4a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\lambda$\" width=\"10\" height=\"13\" /> is defined at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> to be the smallest <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Positive_integer\" title=\"Positive integer\">positive integer</a> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/0/e/40e7de3a2dc1e86c7dbba45c7cdea912079d00f3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\lambda(n)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"34\" height=\"18\" /> such that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/0/1/801d593a866d8fcd1a8c1e3e6848fea87909ade5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a^{\\lambda(n)} \\equiv 1\\pmod {n}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"143\" height=\"20\" /> for all positive <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Integer\" title=\"Integer\">integers</a> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/d/c7d457e388298246adb06c587bccd419ea67f7e8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" /> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Relatively_prime\" title=\"Relatively prime\">relatively prime</a> to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" />. The <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Order_(group_theory)\" title=\"Order (group theory)\">order</a> of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/5/8/95861c05902e375ebaa5976d146e1f42c05b142f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a\\pmod {n}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"82\" height=\"18\" /> always divides <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/0/e/40e7de3a2dc1e86c7dbba45c7cdea912079d00f3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\lambda(n)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"34\" height=\"18\" />.\n</p><p>This function is also known as the <i>reduced totient function</i> or the <i>least universal exponent</i> function. \n</p><p><br />\n</p><p>Suppose <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/2/0/f202d8348df84404299053a6aaf304a00d3e579d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n=p_1^{\\alpha_1}\\cdot p_2^{\\alpha_2}\\cdots p_k^{\\alpha_k}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"149\" height=\"17\" />. We have\n</p>\n<center><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/5/b/b5b3c2fc8c760924368a3668f60be189e679d3df.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\lambda(n) = \\begin{cases}   \\phi(n) &amp;     \\mathrm {for}\\ n=p^{\\alpha},\\ \\mathrm {with}\\ p=2\\ \\mathrm {and}\\ \\alpha\\le 2,\\ \\mathrm {or}\\ p\\ge 3\\\\   \\frac{1}{2}\\phi(n) &amp;     \\mathrm {for}\\ n=2^{\\alpha}\\ \\mathrm {and}\\ \\alpha\\ge 3\\\\   \\mathrm{lcm} (\\lambda(p_1^{\\alpha_1}), \\lambda(p_2^{\\alpha_2}), \\ldots, \\lambda(p_k^{\\alpha_k})) &amp;      \\mathrm{for}\\ \\mathrm{all}\\ n. \\end{cases}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -33px\" width=\"674\" height=\"75\" /></p></center>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Examples\">Examples</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Carmichael_function&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Examples\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Evaluate <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/a/c/0ace5e3e1d4799eb43df02cc7eab3685a8d6d9cb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2009^{2009}\\pmod{1000}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"161\" height=\"19\" />.\n<a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external autonumber\" href=\"http://www.artofproblemsolving.com/Forum/viewtopic.php?p=1363764#1363764\">[1]</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Second_Definition\">Second Definition</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Carmichael_function&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Second Definition\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>The second definition of the Carmichael function is the least common multiples of all the factors of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/d/a/edae70ddedf89e790936ed4035c5ca1480212e0f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\phi(n)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"34\" height=\"18\" />. It is written as <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/d/7/1d793915da21c12718bb96ce5bf00f9b3f8d570b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\lambda&#39;(n)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"39\" height=\"18\" />. However, in the case <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/b/9/2b954889c10ccd3b540695c2825991d3421df4d0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$8|n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"24\" height=\"18\" />, we take <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/f/9/3f9b5975776e06959723a6d0cc83ed75d796bf9c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2^{\\alpha-2}$\" width=\"34\" height=\"15\" /> as a factor instead of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/a/7/7a7680268b8c043f0db7b93f95e529a2f95e6de9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2^{\\alpha-1}$\" width=\"33\" height=\"15\" />.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_also\">See also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Carmichael_function&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: See also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<ul><li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Number_theory\" title=\"Number theory\">Number theory</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Modular_arithmetic\" title=\"Modular arithmetic\">Modular arithmetic</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Euler%27s_totient_theorem\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"Euler&#39;s totient theorem\">Euler's totient theorem</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Carmichael_numbers\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"Carmichael numbers\">Carmichael numbers</a></li></ul>\n<p><i>This article is a stub.  Help us out by <span class=\"plainlinks\"><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/index.php?title=Carmichael_function&amp;action=edit\">expanding it</a></span>.</i>\n</p>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261017234255\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: [show‐toc]\nCPU time usage: 0.018 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.030 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 109/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 298/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 0/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 4/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 3407/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,template)\n100.00%    5.801      1 Template:Stub\n100.00%    5.801      1 -total\n-->\n\n<!-- Saved in parser cache with key wiki-wiki_:pcache:4487:|#|:idhash:canonical and timestamp 20261017234255 and revision id 176505. Rendering was triggered because: page-view\n -->\n</div>"}